;; `apply` through a function *parameter* rather than a name.
;;
;; The restriction that follows from how a rest parameter is represented.
;; `#:rest %a` flattens to an ordinary `(Array %a)` in a function's type, and
;; rest-ness itself is recorded in `FunMeta`, which is keyed by name. So a
;; parameter carries nothing that separates a variadic function from one that
;; genuinely takes an array — and `f` here could be either.
;;
;; Deferring the decision would not help: even once `f`'s metavariable resolves
;; to an arrow, the arrow still does not say which of its parameters was written
;; `#:rest`. There is no later point at which this becomes answerable.

;; EXPECT-ERROR: has no #:rest parameter

(import (std prelude))

(: call-with (-> (-> (Array int) int) (List int) int))
(defun (call-with f xs) (apply f xs))

(defun (main args)
  0)
